  "text": "Let's evaluate the product step by step.\n\nFirst, we will use the change of base formula for logarithms:\n\n\\[\n\\log_a(b) = \\frac{\\log_c(b)}{\\log_c(a)}\n\\]\n\nUsing this formula, we can rewrite each term in the product:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log_k(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log_{k+1}(5^{k^2-4})} = \\frac{\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log(5)}}{\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-4})}{\\log(5)}} = \\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log(5^{k^2-4})}\n\\]\n\nUsing the power rule of logarithms, we can simplify this further:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{\\log(5^{k^2-1})}{\\log(5^{k^2-4})} = \\frac{(k^2-1) \\log(5)}{(k^2-4) \\log(5)} = \\frac{k^2-1}{k^2-4}\n\\]\n\nNow we can rewrite the entire product as:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k^2-1}{k^2-4} \\cdot \\frac{k^2-4}{k^2-9} \\cdot \\frac{k^2-9}{k^2-16} \\cdots \\frac{k^2-3968}{k^2-3969}\n\\]\n\nNotice that this is a telescoping product, where most terms cancel out. The only terms that remain are the first numerator and the last denominator:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{k^2-1}{k^2-3969}\n\\]\n\nNow we need to evaluate this expression at $k=4, 5, 6, \\ldots, 63$:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4^2-1}{4^2-3969} \\cdot \\frac{5^2-1}{5^2-3969} \\cdot \\frac{6^2-1}{6^2-3969} \\cdots \\frac{63^2-1}{63^2-3969}\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{3965} \\cdot \\frac{24}{3964} \\cdot \\frac{35}{3963} \\cdots \\frac{3968}{3969}\n\\]\n\nWe can see that most terms cancel out, leaving us with:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{15}{3969} = \\frac{5}{1323}\n\\]\n\nSo the product is equal to $\\frac{5}{1323}$. Therefore, $m = 5$ and $n = 1323$. Since 5 and 1323 are relatively prime, we can find $m + n$:\n\n\\[\nm + n = 5 + 1323 = 1328\n\\]\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{1328}\n\\]",
